# Copy to .env for local development.

# --- Server-side, and genuinely secret -------------------------------------
# These are read by the Netlify functions at request time. They have no VITE_
# prefix on purpose: Vite inlines any VITE_* variable into the browser bundle,
# which would publish them. `netlify dev` picks them up from this file; plain
# `vite dev` serves no /api/* routes at all, so use mock mode there instead.

# The fal.ai key this deployment generates with. Images, video and the
# "Improve with AI" buttons all run on it, and visitors supply no key of their
# own — which means every generation is billed to whoever sets this.
# FAL_KEY=

# Who may sign in and generate. A comma- or newline-separated list of addresses,
# or "@example.com" for a whole domain. Case-insensitive.
#
# Every provider call runs on the accounts above, so this is the list of people
# allowed to spend them. UNSET MEANS NOBODY: a deployment that has not said who
# it is for refuses every session, loudly, rather than running open until the
# bill arrives. Every function enforces it, so it cannot be got round by calling
# the API directly.
#
# Tell Auth0 the same thing as well, with a Login Action that denies unlisted
# addresses — that stops the sign-in itself, which is a far better experience
# than being let in and refused by every button. See the README.
# ALLOWED_EMAILS=

# The ElevenLabs key this deployment speaks with. It pays for changing a
# recorded voice into another one, and for fixing a clip that mispronounces its
# line: copying the voice out of the clip, then saying the line again properly.
# Billed by the character, roughly $0.10 per 1,000 on the multilingual model —
# a ten-second line is about 2 cents.
#
# There is no key field in the app any more, so this is the only one: without it
# the voice features are unavailable and say so. A copy left in someone's browser
# from the days when there was a field is deleted on their next visit.
#
# The proxy only forwards the handful of endpoints this app calls, refuses to
# delete any voice it did not create, and clears away its own abandoned clones
# when the account runs out of voice slots. See netlify/lib/elevenlabs.ts.
# ELEVENLABS_API_KEY=

# There is no SUPABASE_JWT_SECRET here any more, and setting one does nothing.
# Supabase used to reject Auth0's tokens outright — row-level security only
# trusted JWTs signed with the project's own key — so `/api/session` verified an
# Auth0 token and signed a Supabase-shaped one to replace it. The project now
# registers Auth0 as a third-party auth provider and validates its tokens against
# the tenant's own published keys, so nothing signs anything and no secret is
# involved. Remove it from any deployed environment: it is a live credential that
# can mint a session as anybody, and nothing reads it.
#
# Nothing server-side talks to Supabase either, which is why SUPABASE_URL is gone
# with it. The browser reaches the project directly, under VITE_SUPABASE_URL
# below.

# REQUIRED for Google Drive, which is granted at sign-in rather than after it.
# Auth0 holds the user's Google tokens; these are the credentials the functions
# use to exchange a caller's Auth0 token for a Google one through Token Vault.
#
# These belong to the API's *Custom API Client* — not a machine-to-machine
# application. Access token exchange is the variant where the caller is the
# resource server the token was minted for, and Auth0 decides that by whose
# credentials signed the request: an M2M client answers "This client is not a
# resource server and cannot exchange access tokens", however it is granted.
# Auth0 → Applications → APIs → your API → its Custom API Client.
#
# Its own client rather than the SPA's either way: this call carries a secret and
# the browser must never be able to make it. AUTH0_DOMAIN falls back to
# VITE_AUTH0_DOMAIN below because it is the same tenant regardless; the secret
# has no such fallback, by design.
# AUTH0_BACKEND_CLIENT_ID=
# AUTH0_BACKEND_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Which tenant and which API to verify callers against. Both fall back to their
# VITE_ forms — the same public strings — so these are only worth setting to
# point the functions somewhere else. Tokens are verified locally against the
# tenant's published signing keys, so this costs no round trip per request.
#
# This pair is now what stands between an anonymous request and the fal key:
# /api/fal/* verifies the caller's Auth0 access token against these, where it
# used to verify a session this site had signed. Without either half it refuses
# every request rather than running open.
# AUTH0_DOMAIN=your-tenant.us.auth0.com
# AUTH0_AUDIENCE=https://editor-cat/api

# OPTIONAL, and only for the report bubble in the corner of the editor. Without
# both, /api/github/status answers "not configured" and the bubble says filing is
# not set up here, rather than taking a report someone has written to nowhere.
#
# The token wants to be a *fine-grained* personal access token, scoped to this
# one repository, with Issues: read and write and nothing else. That is the whole
# permission it needs, and so scoped it can do nothing else if it leaks: it
# cannot read code, cannot push, and cannot touch another repository. No VITE_
# prefix, for the usual reason — that would publish it in the bundle.
#
# Filing writes to a public tracker under this deployment's account, so
# /api/github/issues verifies the caller's Auth0 token exactly as /api/fal/* does
# and rate limits per account. The reporter's email address is attached from that
# verified token, never from the request body — which needs the tenant to add a
# https://editor-cat/email claim to its access tokens, or the issue carries the
# Auth0 account id instead. See the README.
# GITHUB_TOKEN=
# GITHUB_REPO=owner/repo

# Skips the session check on /api/fal/* entirely, for running `netlify dev`
# against a checkout with no Auth0 tenant. Never set this on a deployed site: it
# leaves the endpoint open for anyone who finds the URL to generate video on your
# account.
# FAL_PROXY_ALLOW_ANONYMOUS=1

# --- Build-time, and not secret --------------------------------------------
# These ship in the bundle by design. All optional.

# Fakes every provider call locally, so no keys and no network are needed.
# VITE_MOCK_PROVIDERS=1

# REQUIRED for sign-in, and therefore for Drive, which rides on the same login.
# The Auth0 tenant, the SPA application's client id, and the API identifier its
# access tokens are minted for. None is a secret: all three are visible in every
# authorisation request the browser makes.
#
# In Auth0, the application's Allowed Callback URLs, Allowed Logout URLs and
# Allowed Web Origins have to cover wherever this is served from. Unlike Google,
# Auth0 takes a wildcard subdomain — https://*.staging.example.com — which is
# what makes deploy previews work without an entry each. Only ever for a domain
# you own; never a shared one like *.netlify.app, where anyone can deploy a site
# that matches and collect your users' tokens.
# VITE_AUTH0_DOMAIN=your-tenant.us.auth0.com
# VITE_AUTH0_CLIENT_ID=
# VITE_AUTH0_AUDIENCE=https://editor-cat/api

# Google API key and Cloud project number, for the Google Picker — which is how
# the user chooses their folder and imports existing media. Without the key
# there is no way to pick a folder, so the editor cannot open. Neither is a
# secret: restrict the key by HTTP referrer in the Cloud console. Enable the
# "Google Picker API" there alongside the Drive API.
#
# This is the one Google allowlist this app still keeps. The Picker calls Google
# straight from the page, so Auth0 is nowhere in that path and every origin the
# app is served from needs covering — https://*.staging.example.com/* included.
# VITE_GOOGLE_API_KEY=
# VITE_GOOGLE_PROJECT_NUMBER=

# Supabase project, where timelines are saved. Set both to enable cloud
# projects; leave both unset to run purely against this browser's storage.
# The anon key is public by design — row-level security is what protects data.
#
# Two things that cannot be set from here have to be done in a dashboard before
# a signed-in browser can read anything: the project must have Auth0 registered
# under Authentication → Third-Party Auth, and the tenant must run a Login Action
# adding `role: authenticated` to the ID token. Neither is visible to this file
# or to the bundle, and getting either wrong looks like an empty project list
# rather than an error. See the README.
# VITE_SUPABASE_URL=https://xxxxxxxx.supabase.co
# VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=eyJhbGciOi...
